#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110289 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfadce43-b527-59fc-b6c7-ec963bb543b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "09634460-21c4-5389-92f4-15339282b7ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-10455" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac12 e^{x+8}-2$." :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{2}e^{x + 8} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}e^{y + 8} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}e^{y + 8} - 2 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}e^{y + 8} &= x + 2 \\\\\ne^{y + 8} &= 2x + 4 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{y + 8}) &= \\ln (2x + 4) \\\\\ny + 8 &= \\ln (2x + 4) \\\\\ny &= \\ln (2x + 4) - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(2x+4)} - 8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110500 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2efb7d6d-25e9-5bf3-b2b3-5085221b0e76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "fc54368a-099e-5b85-a6c4-15db9be3ee8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4127" :question/problem "What is the inverse of $f(x) =\\ln(x+3)+6$?" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x+3)+6\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\ln (y + 3) + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\ln (y + 3) + 6 \\\\\n\\ln (y + 3) &= x - 6 \\\\\ne^{\\ln (y + 3)} &= e^{x - 6} \\\\\ny + 3 &= e^{x - 6} \\\\\ny &= e^{x - 6} - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = e^{x-6}-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01b24c5-c253-5a11-8055-9be0d60da592" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Exponential Functions With Up to Two Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "2b08436f-c561-59c5-bb79-621a1780b470" :question/math-academy-id "e-4128" :question/problem "The function $f(x) = e^{x-k}+2$ has an inverse function given by $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(x-2)}+4$. What is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= e^{x - k} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= e^{y - k} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= e^{y - k} + 2 \\\\\ne^{y - k} &= x - 2 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{y - k}) &= \\ln (x - 2) \\\\\ny - k &= \\ln (x - 2) \\\\\ny &= \\ln (x - 2) + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(x-2)} + k\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a220ac5f-7b5c-59ef-82d5-aa84654f9fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "c6d0d3ad-111a-5db1-b521-0c9416dfb6b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4140" :question/problem "What is the inverse of $f(x) = 2\\log_4(3x)-1$?" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2\\log_{4} (3x) - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2\\log_{4} (3y) - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2\\log_{4} (3y) - 1 \\\\\n2\\log_{4} (3y) &= x + 1 \\\\\n\\log_{4} (3y) &= \\frac{x + 1}{2} \\\\\n4^{\\log_{4} (3y)} &= 4^{(x + 1)/2} \\\\\n3y &= 4^{(x + 1)/2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{3}4^{(x + 1)/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) =\\dfrac13 4^{(x+1)/2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a220ac5f-7b5c-59ef-82d5-aa84654f9fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Inverses of Logarithmic Functions With More Than Two Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "d7396d89-4bc9-5ba9-b9b1-12012d5351ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107628}] :question/id #uuid "9ae8277e-37c6-59e9-af56-0d7eccfa6c61" :question/math-academy-id "q-228842" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x) = 5\\log_{4} (10x - 1)$ is $f^{-1}(x) =$ {{field-1}}."}]}
